# In Silico — API keys for local review runs.
#
# Copy this to `.env` and paste your key in:
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and `scripts/run_review.py` loads it automatically, so
# nothing needs exporting into your shell. Keys only — model and workflow
# settings live in `peerreview.toml`.
#
# In CI these come from repository secrets instead; this file is for running
# reviews on your own machine.

# --- The one you need ------------------------------------------------------

# Anthropic direct. This is what peerreview.toml is configured for, and what
# the lab key is. Published In Silico reviews run on this.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# --- Optional --------------------------------------------------------------

# OpenRouter, for cheap iteration while tuning prompts or debugging the
# workflow. Pair with the flags rather than editing peerreview.toml:
#
#   python scripts/run_review.py --url ... \
#     --provider openrouter --model minimax/minimax-m3 --debate-rounds 1
#
# ~$0.20/run instead of ~$1.50, on your own key rather than the lab's.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# OpenAI direct — only if you point --provider at it.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- Research layer --------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Novelty and Literature reviewers query arXiv, Semantic Scholar, PubMed,
# and bioRxiv. All work unauthenticated; these only raise rate limits.

# Lifts Semantic Scholar's shared-pool limit. Free: https://www.semanticscholar.org/product/api
# SEMANTIC_SCHOLAR_API_KEY=

# NCBI asks for an email on E-utilities requests, and grants a higher rate
# limit with a key. Free: https://account.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/settings/
# NCBI_EMAIL=
# NCBI_API_KEY=
